:: Henrietta Winetraub [wiki article] {"position":"725,225","size":"100,100"}
<div class="wiki-article">\
<h1>Henrietta Winetraub</h1>\
Henrietta Winetraub is an American musician and media investor. She is known for being the Chairwoman of the Board of (link: "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")[(set: $navPending to "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")(display: "func-navigate")], a media investment firm, where she also led the Music and Audio Entertainment division. While at Recast, she was instrumental in the creation of the (link: "Songs for Family Learning")[(set: $navPending to "Songs for Family Learning")(display: "func-navigate")] album series.

<h2>Early Career</h2>\
Henrietta was a New York-based a songwriter and music producer for a number of public television programs aimed at children in the early 1980s. Her first lead songwriting credit was for a Polish-American musical puppet program called Crusty's Dad Hour. Her 8-song album for the first season was panned in The Musical American for being "didactic and hamfisted," while later seasons were better receieved, with Season 3 even recieving a Ditty Award.

Winetraub met (link: "Tom Barry")[(set: $navPending to "Tom Barry")(display: "func-navigate")] during a heated legal battle regarding a made-for-TV musical adaptation of The Shoemaker's Daughter. Winetraub and the rest of the production team were sued by Barry for the musical's alleged similarities to The Tinker's Daughter, a folk tale features in Barry-owned compilation (link: "Tales of Prior Days")[(set: $navPending to "Tales of Prior Days")(display: "func-navigate")]. The lawsuit ended with Barry recieving an executive producer credit on the film, which aired on public access television in 1985.

<h2>Recast Entertainment Ventures</h2>\
Winetraub, Barry, and (link: "Lawrence Oregon")[(set: $navPending to "Lawrence Oregon")(display: "func-navigate")] opened their joint media investment firm in 1987, with Winetraub serving as Chairwoman of the Board. The firm was incorporated chiefly to produce an animated film based on Witch and The Goldwoman, for which Winetraub would compose the soundtrack.

In 1990, REV began production on a family-friendly album of songs, written by Winetraub and a number of guest musicians, which would eventually become (link: "Songs for Family Learning")[(set: $navPending to "Songs for Family Learning")(display: "func-navigate")]. After the broad commercial success of the first album among parents, teachers, and childrens party entertainers, REV went on to produce 3 follow-up albums in the same series, featuring musical guests such as Kris Kross and Gloria Estafan.

<h2>Later Career</h2>\
After the third SFFL album saw poor commercial returns in 2001, Winetraub was briefly committed to an inpatient mental health facility in Upstate New York.

Her work at REV continued after she was discharged early the following year. A fourth SFFL album was released in 2006.

Winetraub left REV in 2006 to retire in Upstate New York.
</div>

:: Lawrence Oregon [wiki article] {"position":"850,225","size":"100,100"}
<div class="wiki-article">\
<h1>Lawrence Oregon</h1>\
Lawrence Oregon (born c. 1967) is a Canadian business executive. He was the president of (link: "Recast Entertainment Ventures")[(set: $navPending to "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")(display: "func-navigate")], an American media investment firm, from 1994 to 2008. Before Recast, Oregon spent 16 years working in small business management for a series of commercial spiritual organizations.

<h2>Early Life and Education</h2>\
Lawrence Oregon grew up in Huntsville, Ontario, Canada. He received bachelor's degrees in computer science and religious studies from the University of Western Ontario, and an MS in business management from the Glen Cove School of Economics. As a member of the Glen Cove Investors Club, he met (link: "Tom Barry")[(set: $navPending to "Tom Barry")(display: "func-navigate")] and (link: "Henrietta Winetraub")[(set: $navPending to "Henrietta Winetraub")(display: "func-navigate")], with whom he would go on to found REV, with a stated intention to "show America what real entertainment looks like."

<h2>Recast Entertainment Ventures</h2>\
Oregon served as President of the Board of REV, where he spearheaded a number of early investments in toys and entertainment properties, including the (link: "Battlemutts")[(set: $navPending to "Battlemutts")(display: "func-navigate")] franchise. Starting in 2003, he became the head of REV's Entertainment Technologies division, which he used to invest in high-traffic entertainment websites that he could pair with advertising partners. After discovering (link: "Skunkpets")[(set: $navPending to "Skunkpets")(display: "func-navigate")] at (link: "NetCon 2004")[(set: $navPending to "NetCon 2004")(display: "func-navigate")], he connected the game's creators (link: "Krysina")[(set: $navPending to "Anya Krysina")(display: "func-navigate")] and (link: "Galveston")[(set: $navPending to "Nick Galveston")(display: "func-navigate")] with a number of other key investors, and helped them incorporate Skunkpets Inc, of which he owned a majority share. Online ads and a premium ad-free account were added to the game later that year.

<h2>Skunkpets Creative Direction</h2>\
After the founding members of the Skunkpets team left the project in 2009, Oregon became the lead creative director of the game for 3 months. Commonly known as "The Last Season," this period in the game's development was plagued with erratic updates and structural changes, and saw a steep drop in usership.

//This section has been edited and is pending review.//

The website was shut down in May 2009.

<h2>Later Career</h2>\
After REV filed for Chapter 7 bankrupcy in 2009, Lawrence Oregon returned to a prior career position as the spokesperson for a for-profit spiritual organization known as (link: "Saint Detritus Commercial Fellowship")[(set: $navPending to "Saint Detritus Commercial Fellowship")(display: "func-navigate")].
</div>

:: NetCon 2004 [wiki article] {"position":"975,225","size":"100,100"}
<div class="wiki-article">\
<h1>NetCon 2004</h1>\
NetCon 2004 was the third occurance of NetCon, an annual convention for influencers, fans, executives, and online brands. The event primarily features prominent content creators from across the internet. It was founded by veteran YouTube creators Cole Ramsay and Lena Russel (TheSweetpeople), and was later acquired by Hi-Vis (now Paradigm) in 2018. Its offices remain in Montori, Montana, sharing a building with Thinkify. 

<h2>Standout Presenters</h2>\
NetCon's 04 theme was "Pioneer", spotlighting up-and-coming internet companies with innovative online engagement strateiges. Out of 460 panelists and presenters, notable NetCon 04 presences included:
* (link: "Global Games Archive")[(set: $navPending to "globalgamesarchive.co.uk")(display: "func-navigate")], a worldwide online repository for indie games, made their second NetCon appearance this year, hosting a 24-hour game jam for indie developers.
* (link: "Diskette.net")[(set: $navPending to "Diskette")(display: "func-navigate")], a friendly digital office assistant capable of helping users with complex administrative tasks in word processors and spreadsheet managers.
* (link: "Skunkpets.com")[(set: $navPending to "Skunkpets")(display: "func-navigate")], a virtual pet website where users could adopt creatures in the digital realm of Bin City, which had quadrupled its user base since its previous NetCon attendance in 2003.
* ''FindPizzaKid.com'', a semi-parodic website dedicated to a crowdsourced effort to locate Dominic Desantos, a tween boy who was reported missing after appearing in a viral video entitled "Pizza Kid Freaks Out." The website initially featured discussion posts regarding Dominic's whereabouts, but was swiftly taken over as a general community discussion spaces for sharing photo edits, memes, and pizza recommendations.
</div>

:: New Tab [wiki] {"position":"1100,225","size":"100,100"}
<div class="new-tab-page"></div>

:: Nick Galveston [wiki article] {"position":"1225,225","size":"100,100"}
<div class="wiki-article">\
<h1>Nick Galveston (computer scientist)</h1>\
Nickolas Galveston (born 1 April 1982) is an American computer programmer. He was the co-founder of (link: "Skunkpets")[(set: $navPending to "Skunkpets")(display: "func-navigate")], a virtual pet website that was active online 2001-2009.

<h3>Early Life and Education</h3>\
Galveston attended Ellory College in Pennsylvania for 1998-2001, majoring in undergraduate computer science degree. He left college before graduating to work on the Skunkpets project.

<h3>Career</h3>\
Galveston worked on Skunkpets from 2001-2009, after which he left the project, citing 'artistic differences'. Although it was widely speculated that this transition was due to conflicts with the project's leadership at (link: "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")[(set: $navPending to "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")(display: "func-navigate")], Galveston since commented that he 'simply moved on to other projects'.

In 2010, Galveston became a regular lecturer in the Computer Science department at Ellory College, where he still teaches.
</div>

