:: REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures) [wiki article] {"position":"350,350","size":"100,100"}
<div class="wiki-article">\
<h1>REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)</h1>\
<div class="article-art" data-art="rev-storefront.jpg" role="img" aria-label="Recast Entertainment Ventures storefront, Glen Cove"></div>\
Recast Entertainment Ventures was an American media investment firm based in Glen Cove, New York. Originally formed by (link: "Tom Barry")[(set: $navPending to "Tom Barry")(display: "func-navigate")], (link: "Henrietta Winetraub")[(set: $navPending to "Henrietta Winetraub")(display: "func-navigate")] and (link: "Lawrence Oregon")[(set: $navPending to "Lawrence Oregon")(display: "func-navigate")] out of a Glen Cove storefront in 1987, the company established a nationwide development team through a series of media property acquisitions in the late 1990s and early-to-mid 2000s.

<h2>History</h2>\
<h3>1987-1990: Founding</h3>\
<div class="article-art article-art-left" data-art="rev-founders.jpg" style="width: 36%" role="img" aria-label="REV's founders"></div>\
REV was incorporated in 1987, after Tom Barry acquired the adaptation rights to (link: "Tales of Prior Days")[(set: $navPending to "Tales of Prior Days")(display: "func-navigate")], a collection of Eastern-European fairy and folk tales. The firm was formed to oversee dealings with animation studios to produce animated feature films based on the collection. In 1988, REV pitched an animated feature titled Witch and The Goldwoman (based on the folk tale of the same name) to multiple leading animation houses in Hollywood, all of which declined to work on the project. Soon after, REV moved to acquire several more small media properties with an intent to grow their holdings and influence in the evolving media market. All the while, pre-production on Witch and the Goldwoman continued in-house, and Tom Barry stated in 1989 that REV planned to "launch their own animation studio" that would "be a force like nothing America had ever faced before."

<h3>1990-2006: Success</h3>\
In 1990, REV began a steady rollout of project divisions, including toys & games, television programs, and entertainment technologies. Each of these divisions made strategic investments and all-out purchases of upstart entertainment properties which contributed to REV's growth and national-scale expansion. Notable investments during this period included the (link: "Battlemutts")[(set: $navPending to "Battlemutts")(display: "func-navigate")] TV show and toy brand, the (link: "Songs for Family Learning")[(set: $navPending to "Songs for Family Learning")(display: "func-navigate")] CD series, and several home video game consoles. During this period, rumors consistently circulated about the opening of REV's fabled animation studio, with a team of classical animators continuing development on Witch and the Goldwoman, but the project never progressed past the pre-production stage.

Froom 2003-2006, REV's Entertainment Technologies division made moves to expand into the online sector, investing in popular online properties such as (link: "Battlemutts Online")[(set: $navPending to "Battlemutts")(display: "func-navigate")] and (link: "Skunkpets")[(set: $navPending to "Skunkpets")(display: "func-navigate")] virtual pet website. Many of these projects were independently operated, with REV providing fiscal management services, legal counsel, and other corportate-level support. In a 2005 interview, Lawrence Oregon called REV Entertainment Technologies "an open door into the world of tomorrow's entertainment".

<h3>2006-2009: Decline and bankruptcy</h3>\
In 2006, founding board members Barry and Winetraub left REV, reportedly to pursue other venture projects. During the period following their exit, the firm's operations became more erratic and were regularly stalled by internal strategy disputes regarding outsized, overambitious projects. Internal reports suggested that the company was hemmoraging large sums of money to upkeep redundant computer servers. After poor financial returns in their 2008 fiscal year, REV filed for Chapter 7 bankruptcy. Properties owned by REV were subsequently auctioned off to various parties.
</div>

