<h1 id="overview">Overview</h1>
SkunkPets was a browser-based online game that allowed users to create and care for digital pets called "SkunkPets" and explore the virtual realm of Bin City. There was no set objective for the users, but they were expected to feed and care for their SkunkPets when they grew hungry or ill. SkunkPets would not die if neglected, but their health had in-game consequences.

SkunkPets came in a (link: "variety of species and colors")[(set: $navPending to "FanWiki: Skunkpet Types")(display: "func-navigate")] and users could create or adopt their own. SkunkPets could be customised with clothing items, dye jobs, and accessories. Users could build a customisable Burrow for their pet and furnish it with furniture, wallpaper, and flooring. SkunkPets themselves could have mini-pets of their own called (link: "Junkmates")[(set: $navPending to "FanWiki: Junkmates")(display: "func-navigate")].

Bin City was a virtual realm of junkyard-inspired neighborhoods, each of which have their own shops, games, and attractions. It had its own economy and stock market. Players could earn one of two virtual currencies. One currency, called Skunkpoints, could be obtained for free through on-site features like games, events, and contests. The other, SkunkCash (SC), was purchased with real-world money and could be exchanged for in-game souvenirs, including access to restricted game zones and wearable items for pets.

Until 2009, the site was regularly updated with features like new games, items, and content.
</div>

:: Skunkpets [wiki article] {"position":"100,475","size":"100,100"}
<div class="wiki-article"><span class="zoom-trigger" hidden></span>\
<h1>SkunkPets™ (website)</h1>\
<div class="article-art" data-art="skunkpets-6-lw2v.jpg" role="img" aria-label="Skunkpets(6)"></div>
SkunkPets™ is a now-defunct free-to-play virtual pet website. First launched in 2001, the website allowed users to own virtual pets ("SkunkPets") in a virtual realm called Bin City. Players could buy digital food, accessories, and customizations to keep their pets happy. Players could also interact with each other through social features like message boards, guilds, and item trades, and have their pets battle in the "Scrapyard," winning titles and prizes. 

The SkunkPets website was taken down in 2009 following a controversy surrounding the (link: "Order of Refuse")[(set: $navPending to "Order of Refuse")(display: "func-navigate")].

<h2>History and development</h2>\
SkunkPets was conceived in 2000 by (link: "Anya Krysina")[(set: $navPending to "Anya Krysina")(display: "func-navigate")] and (link: "Nick Galveston")[(set: $navPending to "Nick Galveston")(display: "func-navigate")], students at Ellory College. The website was opened for public use on May 1, 2001, and the userbase began to grow through word of mouth.

<h3>NetCon 2004</h3>\
SkunkPets saw a rapid rise in popularity following a successful outing at the (link: "2004 NetCon")[(set: $navPending to "NetCon 2004")(display: "func-navigate")] convention in San Francisco. SkunkPets was the darling of the convention, its user count quadrupling by the end of the weekend.

<h3>Growth & Corporate Ownership (2004-2008)</h3>\
By December 2004, SkunkPets was logging 400,000 page views daily and sought investors to cover the high cost of running the site. American businessman (link: "Lawrence Oregon")[(set: $navPending to "Lawrence Oregon")(display: "func-navigate")] was introduced to the team and bought a majority share on behalf of (link: "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")[(set: $navPending to "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")(display: "func-navigate")]. In October, SkunkPets released a premium version and started showing advertisements on the basic site that were not shown to premium members.

SkunkPets physical merchandise became available shortly thereafter, usually containing a code which could be redeemed on the site for an in-game reward. In 2006, Oregon said that SkunkPets had an opportunity to "bring the online and offline worlds together in a way never before seen".

In 2008, news leaked that a SkunkPets animated television series was in production at a major kids television network (speculated to be Nickelodeon). The project was eventually cancelled due to studio conflicts with REV.

<h3>Financial Struggles & Discontinuation (2007-2009)</h3>\
As early as December 2006, SkunkPets was plagued by user complaints regarding both functionality and added content. Despite a steady influx of advertising income and premium memberships, there were reports of mismanagement and development gridlock stemming from internal disagreements. 

In March 2009, several core team members including Nick Galveston left the company, citing artistic differences. In May 2009, the game's official website was shut down. The player community remained active for some time after on unofficial platforms like fan-made forums and wiki sites(link: "^^1^^")[(set: $navPending to "Skunkpets Fan Wiki")(display: "func-navigate")].

<h2>Controversy</h2>\
//This section has been edited and is pending review.//

</div>

:: Songs for Family Learning [wiki article] {"position":"225,475","size":"100,100"}
<div class="wiki-article">\
<h1>Songs for Family Learning</h1>\
<div class="article-art" data-art="songs-for-family-learning.jpg" role="img" aria-label="Songs for Family Learning logo"></div>\
“Songs for Family Learning” is a series of albums released by (link: "Recast Entertainment Ventures")[(set: $navPending to "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")(display: "func-navigate")]  from 1992 to 2006. The albums featured compilations of songs by various artists, primarily consisting of educational and instructional music for young children. The albums were released on CD and magnetic cassette, with special vinyl editions made available for the series’ 10th anniversary.

The unofficial slogan of the series, “make being together a good time!” resurfaced as a viral internet meme and inspired a popular line of ironic T-shirts which led to an infamous contentious lawsuit.

<h2>Discography</h2>\
The release dates mentioned pertain to the date of release in the United States.\
<table class="wikitable">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 55%;">Track Title</th>
      <th style="width: 15%;">Volume</th>
      <th style="width: 30%;">Release Date</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Hum and Whistle</td>
      <td>SFFL #1</td>
      <td>June 1st, 1992</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>The Best Way (With A Friend)</td>
      <td>SFFL #1</td>
      <td>June 1st, 1992</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Everybody in the Neighborhood</td>
      <td>SFFL #1</td>
      <td>June 1st, 1992</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>I Do My Dishes Every Day</td>
      <td>SFFL #1</td>
      <td>June 1st, 1992</td>
    </tr>
