:: Tales of Prior Days [wiki article] {"position":"350,475","size":"100,100"}
<div class="wiki-article">\
<h1>Tales of Prior Days</h1>\
Tales of Prior Days `(Russian: Истории Прошлих Дней, variously translated; English titles include also Long Ago Stories)` is a collection of nearly 300 fairy and folktales, collected and published by Dmitry Kanisev between 1860 and 1879. The collection contained fairy and folk tales from Ukraine, Belarus, Poland, Moldova, Latvia, and Serbia.

Some of the tales included in these volumes:
* The Wife of the Dead Soldier
* The Tinker's Daughter
* The Snake and the Hunter
* Unlucky Princess
* The House With Beaks
* The Wicked Ass and the Broom
* Simpleton
* Witch and the Goldwoman
* The Pin Keeper
* The Mother on Fire
* Tsarevich Igor and the Fat Wolf
* Go, Go, Go, Go, Go, Go!
* The Haughty Boy, the Golden Apple, and the Wicker Basket
* Go I Know Not Whither and Fetch I Know Not What
* The Girl of the Cliffs
* Candle, Candle, Burn!
* Great Old Radish
* Sisters Morning, Noon, and Night
* The Sleepy Murderer
* The Language of the Pigs
* Hot Milk
* The Girl Who Spoke To Birch Trees
* Salko-Balko
* War Bear
<h2>References</h2>\
Silverman, Barbara R. (1997). Kononenko, Natalie O. (ed.). Tsarevich Ivan and the Fat Wolf, and other tales from Latvia. World folklore series. Englewood, Colo: Libr. Unlimited. pp. xxi. Halsin, Jack M. (2014). "Introduction: D.I. Kanisev His Life and Works". In Halsin, Jack M. (ed.). The Complete Folktales of D.I. Kanisev. Vol. I.: University Press of Michigan. pp. xxiv, xxvii. Barry Family Record (2001). Article XI, "Witch and the Goldwoman and Other Tales".
</div>

:: Tom Barry [wiki article] {"position":"475,475","size":"100,100"}
<div class="wiki-article">\
<h1>Tom Barry</h1>\
Tom Barry //(born Thomas Melnyk)// is an American businessman and media moghul, known for his work with (link: "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")[(set: $navPending to "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")(display: "func-navigate")] and his legacy in the legal side of fairy tale media adaptations. He grew up in Cleveland, Ohio, son of Elys Melnyk and Melina Barrarro, both copyright lawyers. When both parents were killed in an industrial accident, fourteen-year-old Thomas moved to Upstate New York to live with his cousins until he was of age to manage the family trust.

<h2>Early life and career</h2>\
At eighteen, Thomas (now Tom Barry) came into legal possession of all the intellectual property his parents had come to own throughout their careers, including the sole publishing and adaptation rights for several historic manuscripts, which formed the bulk of his early media investment portfolio. One of these books, (link: "Tales of Prior Days")[(set: $navPending to "Tales of Prior Days")(display: "func-navigate")], was a collection of eastern-European folktales published under unique names, but with many bearing similarities to other collected tales from the region.

After getting his legal license from a correspondance school, Tom Barry spent the next two decades aggressively litigating the adaptation rights for  fairy tale similarities with a number of publishing houses, comics companies, and film & animation studios. Among these cases was Barry v. Miracle Motion Pictures, popularly called "The Fat Wolf Case." This suit established legal precedent that commercial media use of the term "fat wolf" constituted an intellectual property infringement, due to the term's appearance in the Barry-owned folk tale //Tsarevich Igor and the Fat Wolf//.

<h2>Recast Entertaainment Ventures</h2>\
Barry's many intellectual property lawsuits yielded the seed funding for (link: "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")[(set: $navPending to "REV (Recast Entertainment Ventures)")(display: "func-navigate")], which he founded out of a Glen Cove storefront with (link: "Henrietta Winetraub")[(set: $navPending to "Henrietta Winetraub")(display: "func-navigate")] and (link: "Lawrence Oregon")[(set: $navPending to "Lawrence Oregon")(display: "func-navigate")] in 1987. Recast's stated business plan was to build a diversified media property portfolio including film, television and games. During his tenure at Recast, Barry's longterm focus was on a fabled feature-length animated adaptation of folk tale //Witch and the Goldwoman//, which he oversaw through various stages (including its failed Hollywood pitch circuit and its longtime in-house development). He continued to actively work on the Witch and Goldwoman project, which was in and out of pre-production, until he left Recast in 2006.

<h2>Later career</h2>\
After leaving Recast, Barry made several attempts to purchase small subsections of major animation studios, likely to complete his still-unproduced Goldwoman film. During this time, he reportedly contacted the head of animal reference photography at Disney Animation to inquire whether "the animal guys over there `[were]` ready for something `[explative]` serious yet." Barry continues to work independently as a media investor and intellectual property lawyer at his firm Tom Barry Associates, which he manages with his two sons.
</div>

